[17592186180387 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180390 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180390 #edb/ref 1061 23747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180390 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6acb0f4b-5b56-5e4c-a728-4db2fd840bff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180390 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180492 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180492 #edb/ref 1061 35726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180492 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c7465601-d18a-5a90-bfe9-a27fe52c871f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180492 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180501 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180501 #edb/ref 1061 35769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180501 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4246c091-bba6-587e-8d43-72e5c01a1e08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180501 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180678 #edb/ref 1064 "Since there is a fundamental link between line integrals of vector-valued functions and line integrals with respect to $x$ and $y,$ it should be unsurprising that they share similar properties. We list two of them below:\n\n- If $-C$ denotes the path obtained by reversing the orientation of $C,$ then\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r = -\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\n- If $C = C_1\\cup C_2$ is the union of two paths $C_1$ and $C_2$, then\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r \n= \n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r + \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\nThe first property has an intuitive meaning if we think about it in terms of the work done by a force on a particle. To see this, consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ shown below. Let $C$ be the path defined by traversing the line segment from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/4f/4f69f80d98d301410908f7ec867a89bdc834c22a51f224d9b00b80ceca39ab87.png)\n\n \n\nLet $W(C)$ be the work done in moving the particle along $C.$ Since our particle moves in the same direction as the vector field, we have\n\n$$\nW(C) = \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r > 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if we move the particle from $B$ to $A,$ we expect the line integral to be negative. This is because the particle is now moving against $\\mathbf F.$ \n\n![](images/fe/fe6b2e67d33a4993104406c88b1dc0dc897be33d034007ed581a75f741ddf620.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW(-C) = \\int\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r < 0.\n$$\n\nWe can show that the work done in moving the particle along $-C$ is the same as the work done in moving it along $C$ but with the opposite sign. That is\n\n$$\nW(-C) = -W(C).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r = -\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180678 #edb/ref 1065 3976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180678 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d745eed3-7e69-55af-b685-1c7482dfffb9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180678 #edb/ref 1067 "Reversing the Orientation of a Curve and Sums Over Unions of Paths" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180722 #edb/ref 1064 "Line integrals of vector-valued functions share a fundamental connection with line integrals with respect to $x$ and $y.$\n\nConsider a vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^2$ with components $P$ and $Q\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nNow suppose that $C$ is a smooth curve on $\\mathbb R^2,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r (t) = x(t)\\,\\mathbf i + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [a,b].\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the line integral of $\\mathbf F$ along $C$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}(P(x(t),y(t))\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+Q(x(t),y(t))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}P(x(t),y(t))\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+Q(x(t),y(t))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{C}P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r  = \\int\\limits_C P(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x + Q(x,y)  \\,\\textrm d y\n$$\n\nAn analogous result holds for vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3.$ That is, \n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r  = \\int\\limits_C P(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm d x + Q(x,y,z)  \\,\\textrm d y + R(x,y,z) \\,\\textrm d z\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = P(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j + R(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180722 #edb/ref 1065 5837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180722 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b4ca8cae-81df-5c55-90ac-839b063e78a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180722 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324341 #edb/ref 1080 "e-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324341 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324341 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2e6cd156-8ca4-5855-a3cc-5f0d395f4aad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324370 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{3}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324370 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324370 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "317a4675-7255-5700-9ad9-8cfade1093ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324842 #edb/ref 1080 "2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324842 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324842 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "68507ca4-abf8-5cd6-8193-9965c1c024dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325504 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325504 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325504 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b2a34229-1bb6-551f-bdc6-3c2c3f4134b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325768 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{7}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325768 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325768 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ceeccb5b-f4c0-5768-a9a9-bdc8a37556ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326223 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186324341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326223 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186324370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326223 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186324842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326223 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186325504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326223 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186325768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326223 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326223 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326223 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186325504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326223 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "01d160d2-e817-535b-93be-44d3599c92c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326775 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326775 #edb/ref 1069 "q-39673" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326775 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326775 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/73/73c6a8268a179f72611fb65e099d1249d48501e4fd4731d03747800e1944fd97.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(1,2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(1,2)$ to the point $D(1,3).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{7}{2},$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(2x-3y)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326775 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c31eda49-a231-50f2-81e8-83c448690ed2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326775 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is the line segment from $A$ to $B.$  It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩+t(⟨1,2⟩-⟨0,1⟩) \\\\  & =⟨t,1+t⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,i+(1+t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=t$ and $y=1+t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =(2x-3y)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t-3(1+t))\\,i+(t+(1+t))\\,j \\\\  & =(-3-t)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =((-3-t)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j)\\cdot (i+j) \\\\  & =-3-t+2t+1 \\\\  & =t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-2)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-2t]}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}-2-0]+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342257 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342257 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342257 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "013ef795-942d-50f4-83dc-15aab4fbe0c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342511 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{8}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342511 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342511 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "21279c87-66bb-50e4-8d0a-b4f4a20df582" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342736 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342736 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342736 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3c789914-0f4b-5e38-a6be-25a97983968e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342761 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342761 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342761 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3f99a0c2-ff39-555f-979d-c5dceb67d68c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342954 #edb/ref 1080 "-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342954 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342954 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "561cf640-b717-5a0c-a5a7-05b94a807500" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342960 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{8}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342960 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342960 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "56b72719-148e-5b84-8585-452f45bb273d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343009 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{5}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343009 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343009 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5c203805-aaee-5456-a95d-7a12caeeb4c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343128 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343128 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343128 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "683b017e-1f61-5d0d-8485-fec00b137cb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343615 #edb/ref 1080 "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\te